From aca34d755111fe76576dc19e00e18df491a8d648 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Juan=20Manuel=20L=C3=B3pez=20Carrillo?=
 <juanmanuellopezcarrillo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 19:25:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 129/143] media: sunxi: cedar-ve: don't release proc_info lock
 a caller never took
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ve_debug_proc_info.lock_proc is held across a pair of ioctls: it is taken
by IOCTL_SET_PROC_INFO and released by IOCTL_COPY_PROC_INFO.  COPY_PROC_INFO
unconditionally does mutex_unlock(&ve_debug_proc_info.lock_proc) on every
exit path, even when the calling fd never issued a SET_PROC_INFO and thus
never acquired the lock.

A task that opens /dev/cedar_dev and calls IOCTL_COPY_PROC_INFO directly
(CONFIG_DEBUG_FS enabled) therefore unlocks a mutex it does not own.  If
another fd is mid-sequence holding lock_proc via SET_PROC_INFO, this clears
that owner and lets a second writer into the shared ve_debug_proc_info
critical section; with CONFIG_DEBUG_MUTEXES it trips the owner assertion.

Gate the release on the fd actually holding VE_LOCK_PROC_INFO, mirroring the
IOCTL_RELEASE_LOCK check.  The libcedarc contract always pairs SET_PROC_INFO
and COPY_PROC_INFO on the same fd, so it is unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Juan Manuel López Carrillo <juanmanuellopezcarrillo@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: AI Agent
---
 drivers/media/platform/sunxi/cedar-ve/cedar_ve.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/cedar-ve/cedar_ve.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/cedar-ve/cedar_ve.c
index 37609820a..36296606e 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/cedar-ve/cedar_ve.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/cedar-ve/cedar_ve.c
@@ -1914,6 +1914,17 @@ static long compat_cedardev_ioctl(struct file *filp, unsigned int cmd, unsigned
 				if (cedar_devp->debug_root == NULL)
 					return 0;
 
+				/*
+				 * lock_proc is acquired by IOCTL_SET_PROC_INFO and
+				 * released here.  If this fd never issued a matching
+				 * SET_PROC_INFO it does not hold lock_proc, so bail out
+				 * before touching the shared state or unlocking a mutex
+				 * we do not own (which would corrupt another fd's
+				 * critical section).
+				 */
+				if (!(vi->lock_flags & lock_type))
+					return -EPERM;
+
 				mutex_lock(&vi->lock_flag_io);
 				vi->lock_flags &= (~lock_type);
 				mutex_unlock(&vi->lock_flag_io);
